.TH STARLING 1
.SH NAME
starling \- a fixed shooter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/starling
[
.B -t
|
.B -s
|
.B -b
] [
.B -q
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Starling
is a shooter in the shape that genre took:
a ship at the bottom of the screen that only moves sideways,
attackers that fly in along a curve, settle into a swaying formation of
eight columns by five rows, and peel off it one at a time to dive at you
and drop something on the way past.
A diver shot is worth double.
Clearing the formation ends the wave and the next one dives sooner.
.PP
Of the three games on
.IR tiles (2)
this is the one that tests how many sprites the library will put on a
screen: a full formation is forty 16 by 16 characters, which is a
hundred and sixty tile blits a frame before the shots and the
forty-eight stars.
.B -b
measures exactly that; see DIAGNOSTICS.
.PP
Arrow keys or
.B wasd
to move,
.BR x ,
.B .
or space to fire, two shots in the air at a time.
.B q
or Escape quits.
Run it with
.B /env/INFERNO_FPS
set and
.IR frameclock (2)
reports the rate it really managed on the way out.
.PP
.B -q
starts silent.
.B -t
runs the self test.
.B -s
plays a scripted opening headless and writes the frame to
.BR starling.raw .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
games/starling
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/starling.dis \-b
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/starling.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tiles (2),
.IR rivet (1),
.IR sprocket (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B -b
settles a full formation with the dives held off and times 300 frames of
step and draw into memory, with no window and no frame pacing, so the
number is the work and not the wait.
On this host that is about 210 microseconds a frame with the JIT and 283
without \- forty attackers, ninety-six shots' worth of slots and
forty-eight stars, against a 60Hz budget of 16666.
It says
.B SLOWER THAN 60Hz
if it ever stops being true.
.PP
.B -t
drives the same
.I step
the game loop drives and asserts on game state.
It checks that a full formation enters and that every attacker reaches
its slot, that the slots are one column and one row apart and sway
together without leaving the screen, that the ship moves and is stopped
by both walls, that firing is limited to two shots at once, that a hit
starts an explosion and removes an attacker and scores, that a dive
leaves the formation and comes back round the top, that a bomb and a
collision each cost a ship, and that an empty formation clears the wave
and speeds the next one up.
.PP
The wave generator is a small linear congruential one seeded from a
constant under
.BR -t ,
because a test that plays a different game every run is not a test of
anything in particular.
Playing seeds it from the clock.
.SH BUGS
The dives are held off while the test sets a formation up.
Without that the test sits still under attack for six hundred frames,
loses every ship, and then measures a ship that cannot move because
.I step
is in its dead branch \- which reads as eight unrelated failures rather
than as one.
It uses the game's own
.I divecool
to do it; nothing test-only was added to the game.
.PP
There is one formation shape.
Later waves differ only in how often an attacker dives.
.PP
A diver steers towards the ship weakly and by a fixed acceleration, so
it is a threat rather than a pattern; nothing here reproduces a flight
path laid down as data, which is how the arcade did it.
